## Backups

The cron Worker dumps every table of the database to the backups bucket each day: one NDJSON object per table (more when a table is large) and a `manifest.json`, under `backups/<date>/<time>/`. `_cf_*` and `sqlite_*` tables are left out (D1 refuses to read the first). The media are not copied: they are already in the media bucket, and the `media` table of the dump is their index. Backups older than `BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS` (30, a var of `apps/cron/wrangler.jsonc`) are deleted by the next run; a value that is not a whole number of days, 1 or more, stops the run before it writes or deletes anything. D1 Time Travel rewinds the whole database to any point of the last 30 days and covers nothing of R2.

A run reads the tables in pages of 2,000 rows, one D1 query per page, in one invocation. D1 counts the queries of an invocation (1,000 on the Workers Paid plan, 50 on the Free plan), so a database of more than about two million rows in total needs a run split by table.

To run one now, with the token you stored at the first deploy (`BACKUP_TRIGGER_TOKEN`; Cloudflare never shows a secret again, so set a new one with `wrangler secret put` if you lost it):

```bash
curl -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $BACKUP_TRIGGER_TOKEN" https://{{projectName}}-cron.<subdomain>.workers.dev/backup/run
```

Locally, `{{#inDirectory "apps" "cron"}}{{runScript "dev"}}{{/inDirectory}}` serves the Worker on port {{appPort "cron"}} over the same local state as the web app, and the same request with the token of `apps/cron/.env` writes a backup to the local backups bucket. Fire the schedule itself, without waiting for 03:00: `curl -X POST "http://localhost:{{appPort "cron"}}/cdn-cgi/local/explorer/api/local/scheduled?worker={{projectName}}-cron" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"cron":"0 3 * * *"}'`.

